owing we move our  right shoulder left hip and we have a  connection across the body between these  two different sides when you add an  ipsilateral load this will try and pull  towards the leg that's stable on the  ground and this is going to engage the  glute maximus more the hip abductor so  it's a nice way to mix up what you're  training specifically also allows you to  add a little bit more weight it's a  little bit easier for those starting out  and that lets you target the hamstrings  more so if you're keen on those benefits  then you should do this too  or of course you can perform this with  no weights whatsoever and just use your  arms sticking out one or both of them  and this is fantastic because it lets  you practice this movement literally  anywhere some people also like to use a  land mine which can help to reduce the  stability challenge or alternatively if  you struggle a lot to balance and you  don't want to add any weight at all you  can just use an arm to support yourself  on the wall to begin with or even put  your foot out to touch something behind  you just make sure that you're mindful  of those hips that pelvis and keeping it  straight because that's really the whole  point  but why should we be training with the  single leg rdl what are the benefits  well first of all the single leg rdl is  of course fantastic for hip  stabilization when you lean down on one  leg you need to try and keep your pelvis  straight and this is one of the most  important cues when you're performing  this movement you need to make sure that  your pelvis is in line with the ground  you don't want it to be opening up to  one side as your body twists that's not  really a single leg rdl this then  requires you to utilize the gluteus  medius with the contralateral quadratus  lumborum which is a lot of big words  basically contralateral means on the  other side so you're utilizing that  small muscle in the buttocks in the  glutes along with the muscle that  connects your spine to your pelvis  in other words you're using muscles on  either side of your body to keep your  pelvis aligned as you bend down and this  is really important because this is the  same stability you need when you're  running when you're jumping when you do  anything off of one leg when you're  throwing a roundhouse kick as you age  developing strength and coordination in  these muscles will help to prevent you  from falling if you're an athlete having  more stability in this area will mean  that less energy is wasted the more of  it's transferred into the ground  allowing you to spring off optimally and  run as fast as possible for as long as  possible training the quadratus lumborum  can also help a lot of people dealing  with back pain and it's also really good  for things like farmers walks  particularly if you're loading it on one  side building up those big muscles that  are so often overlooked can help to keep  your core nice and rigid against force  another really good reason to train with  a single leg rdl is that it's a hip  hinge movement it teaches the hip hinge  and then it trains and strengthens you  in that pattern so the hip hinge of  course means that you're bending over  but instead of bending your back you're  instead keeping your back straight and  hinging at the hips hence the name you  can practice this by performing butt  touches where you stand against a wall  or tree or something and then just try  and move your butt backwards to touch  the wall without moving anything else  that's a hip hinge and this is what we  should be doing when we bend over to  pick something up off the ground but of  course what a lot of us do is bend our  spines and that can lead to injury of  course it's okay to bend your spine from  time to time that's a whole different  conversation but the optimal movement  most of the time is the hip hinge so you  want to train this  in order to hip hinge properly you're  also going to want to contract the  glutes if you don't feel it in your  glutes then you might not be using them  properly and i think this is a great  movement to use following a set of  barbell hip thrusters or perhaps a glute  bridge this way you can activate the  glutes and then feel them working and  you should really tense them to push  yourself forwards and again this helps  you to drive power from the right place  to prevent injury the single leg rdl  might seem like a bit of a silly  movement if you're used to really heavy  deadlifts but by targeting the hamstring  more and the stability of your hips you  can actually feel it with relatively  light weight it's surprising how much  this burns the hamstrings the next day  if you're not used to it and you can  also feel it in those hip just like  you'll really feel the romanian deadlift  itself in the hamstrings if you haven't  done it a lot of course you're also  training your balance here your  vestibular system your proprioception  and your ankle strength and the strength  in the muscles of your lower leg this is  of course again going to prevent you  from twisting your ankle or injuring  yourself whether playing sports or just  moving through life check out my video  on ankle strength if you want to learn  more about strengthening those muscles  and preventing twisted ankles and falls  and will certainly be returning to  balance again in the future soon  at the same time training the hip hinge  movement is what's going to stretch and  strengthen the hamstrings this is really  important because the hamstrings are so  often overlooked in the gym and  especially in more compound movements  where they're working in tandem with  other muscles this is one of the reasons  that hamstring injuries are among the  most common amongst professional  athletes in sprinting cycling and many  other activities extension at the upper  leg is actually more important a lot of  the time than flexion at the knee so we  want to be able to drive a lot of power  through here if we're going to perform  optimally stretching those hamstrings is  also going to mean that you don't round  your back when you bend over which can  eventually lead to injuries and problems  this is a fantastic way to restore that  mobility that's so many of us have lost  from sitting at desk jobs all the time  and the great thing is in terms of  preventing back injuries is that this is  also directly training the spine because  you have to keep your spine straight  against the weight that's hanging in  front of you to do this of course you're  going to need the spinal erectors but  also the multifidus muscles the rotators  and you can be using your core muscles  in an anti-rotation capacity to prevent  your torso from twisting because that  weight's just on one side so you're  doing all this at the same time to build  a really stable strong core and proper  biomechanics so the whole posterior  chain  thanks a ton for watching guys hope you  found this video useful interesting if  you did then please leave a like and  share around that'd help me out  immensely let me know in the comments  down below whether you use the single  leg rdl do you prefer the contralateral  one or the ipsilateral one do you prefer  doing it with body weight or perhaps a  barbell let me know if you enjoy this  type of training that looks at not only  strength and aesthetics but also things  like balance speed mobility even focus  and brain power you might enjoy my ebook  and training program super functional  training 2.0 which is a program designed  to build all those things all at once in  the most efficient way possible it comes  with over two hours of video tutorial an  80 plus page ebook and loads of extras  i'll leave the link to that in  description down below either way thank  you so much for watching this one guys i  massively appreciate it and i'll see you  next time  bye for now

